  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "One transaction",
      "items": [
        "the mutation and its outbox row commit together in GRDB — the app can be killed between the tap and the network and neither is lost",
        "the row carries an idempotency key, so a retry after a timeout does not archive twice",
        "the optimistic local state carries a rollback: if the row never lands, the screen returns to the server's truth"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "What wakes the drain",
      "items": [
        "three triggers: the network reconnects, the app returns to the foreground, and a background task runs while the app is suspended",
        "the drain reads a bounded batch and sends them out of the main actor — a GRDB query on the main actor is a review finding",
        "a failed row is retried with backoff; once the attempts are exhausted it lands in a dead-letter view a human can act on"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "The server wins",
      "items": [
        "a 409 is resolved server-side: the local pending change is rejected visibly, never silently overwritten",
        "the rejected mutation returns to the screen as a rendered state, so the user sees that their change did not land",
        "sign out or a tenant switch clears the outbox together with the rest of the user data"
      ]
    }
  ]
